// Unit 5 Pretest — 10 questions, diagnostic only

::U5PRE Q01::A fictional person has severe breathing difficulty. What is the priority? {=Immediate emergency assistance ~Wait for a routine appointment ~Complete a course assignment first ~Ask peers to diagnose}

::U5PRE Q02::Exposure to a communicable disease {=does not by itself prove infection ~always proves infection ~is the same as diagnosis ~means treatment is never needed}

::U5PRE Q03::Family history of chronic disease {=may affect risk but does not guarantee an outcome ~guarantees disease ~makes prevention useless ~is a diagnosis}

::U5PRE Q04::A screening result {=may indicate a need for professional evaluation ~is always a final diagnosis ~proves personal blame ~replaces follow-up}

::U5PRE Q05::Maintained abstinence from sexual intercourse {=prevents pregnancy resulting from intercourse ~makes consent unnecessary ~proves STI status ~is determined by peers}

::U5PRE Q06::Which statement is accurate? {=Some pregnancy-prevention methods do not protect against STIs ~Every contraceptive prevents STIs ~No symptoms prove no STI ~Appearance reveals infection}

::U5PRE Q07::Secrecy, isolation, and threats in a fictional opportunity should lead a student to {=disengage and seek appropriate trusted help ~investigate personally ~arrange a private meeting ~accuse publicly}

::U5PRE Q08::A warning sign of exploitation {=raises concern but is not automatic proof ~always proves trafficking ~requires confrontation ~should be ignored}

::U5PRE Q09::A routine preventive screening is generally best matched first with {=an appropriate qualified preventive or primary-care service ~an emergency department in every case ~an anonymous seller ~no service}

::U5PRE Q10::Which is the strongest healthcare credibility indicator? {=Verified credentials, evidence, transparency, and realistic claims ~A guaranteed cure ~Immediate payment pressure ~A professional-looking advertisement alone}

